C = [
("h1", "11. The First Man and the Last Adam"),
("p", "Claim: 1 Corinthians 15:45–49 is the second pairing of Adam and Christ at full length, and it is built on the pair of adjectives set in verse 44, natural and spiritual. The passage says what each man is, in what order they come, what each is made of or from, and what those who belong to each bear. It speaks of a body and an image, and not of an offence and a gift."),
("sub", "11.1 “A living soul” and “a quickening spirit” (15:45)"),
("p", "“And so it is written, The first man Adam was made a living soul; the last Adam was made a quickening spirit” (1 Corinthians 15:45). The verse quotes Scripture for the first half only. The words “the first man Adam was made a living soul” take up the record of the creation of man: “the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul” (Genesis 2:7). The second half of the verse, “the last Adam was made a quickening spirit,” is Paul’s own statement, set beside the first half in the same form."),
("p", "The two halves have the same grammar and different contents. Adam was made “a living soul,” a creature who is alive. The last Adam was made “a quickening spirit,” a spirit who makes alive. The first receives life, and the second gives it. The name “last” does not mean that there is no later man. It means that the man so named closes the line that began with the first, and that no one else will stand in that place."),
("p", "Genesis 2:7 is here only as background. The volume does not take up the creation record for its own sake, does not date it, and does not say more of Adam than Paul does. The verse in Genesis is named because Paul names it, and it is read as he reads it: as the account of how the first man became a living creature."),
("sub", "11.2 The order (15:46)"),
("p", "“Howbeit that was not first which is spiritual, but that which is natural; and afterward that which is spiritual” (1 Corinthians 15:46). The order is plain. The natural comes first and the spiritual follows. This is the order of the two men, and the order of the two kinds of body. It is also the order of the sown and the raised in verses 42 to 44: sown natural, raised spiritual. The verse says that the spiritual does not come first. A reader who expected the resurrection body to be the original and the present body a falling away from it finds the opposite. The present body is first, and the resurrection body is added to it."),
("sub", "11.3 The earthy and the heavenly (15:47)"),
("p", "“The first man is of the earth, earthy: the second man is the Lord from heaven” (1 Corinthians 15:47). The two men are now described by their origin. The first is “of the earth, earthy,” a phrase that recalls the man formed “of the dust of the ground” (Genesis 2:7), and the sentence pronounced on him: “for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return” (Genesis 3:19). The second man is “the Lord from heaven.” The title is the highest in the passage. He is called a man, the second man, and in the same breath the Lord."),
("ctbl", "The two men in 1 Corinthians 15:45–47.", [
    ("The first man", "The second man, the last Adam", "Where it is said"),
    ("“the first man Adam”", "“the last Adam”", "15:45"),
    ("“was made a living soul”", "“was made a quickening spirit”", "15:45"),
    ("“that which is natural” comes first", "“that which is spiritual” comes afterward", "15:46"),
    ("“of the earth, earthy”", "“the Lord from heaven”", "15:47"),
  ], [0.34, 0.40, 0.26]),
("sub", "11.4 Those who belong to each (15:48–49)"),
("p", "“As is the earthy, such are they also that are earthy: and as is the heavenly, such are they also that are heavenly” (1 Corinthians 15:48). The verse turns from the two men to those who belong to each. Those who are earthy are like the earthy man. Those who are heavenly are like the heavenly man. Each head stamps his own character on those who are joined to him."),
("p", "“And as we have borne the image of the earthy, we shall also bear the image of the heavenly” (1 Corinthians 15:49). Paul says “we.” He writes as one of those who are to bear the image. The tenses are pointed: “have borne” is past, and “shall…bear” is future. The Corinthians have borne the image of the first man, and they shall bear the image of the second. The background in Genesis is the same record that speaks of image. Adam “begat a son in his own likeness, after his image” (Genesis 5:3), and the line of the first man is a line that bears his image."),
("p", "The verse says what the resurrection is for those whom Paul addresses. It is a change of image, from the earthy to the heavenly. The volume reads the company of these verses as the company of verse 23, the ones who are Christ’s, and it reads the heavenly image as the image of their glorification, following Volume 4 of the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series, The Resurrection Order in Scripture. That volume reads the verses as speaking of the heavenly glory of the Body of Christ. This volume does not set out the grounds again. It takes the reading and tests it against the verses."),
("sub", "11.5 What Paul says elsewhere of the house from heaven"),
("p", "The same writer says something close to this in another letter. “For we know that if our earthly house of this tabernacle were dissolved, we have a building of God, an house not made with hands, eternal in the heavens” (2 Corinthians 5:1). “For in this we groan, earnestly desiring to be clothed upon with our house which is from heaven” (2 Corinthians 5:2). The words differ, and the picture is a different one: a house, not a seed. Yet the direction is the same. What is now is earthly and dissolving. What is to come is “from heaven” and “eternal in the heavens.” The two letters agree in giving the future body a heavenly character."),
("tbl", "1 Corinthians 15:45–49, verse by verse.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("1Cor 15:45", "“The first man Adam was made a living soul; the last Adam was made a quickening spirit.”", "The first receives life; the last gives it"),
    ("1Cor 15:46", "“that was not first which is spiritual, but that which is natural; and afterward that which is spiritual”", "The natural first, the spiritual after"),
    ("1Cor 15:47", "“The first man is of the earth, earthy: the second man is the Lord from heaven.”", "Two origins"),
    ("1Cor 15:48", "“As is the earthy, such are they also that are earthy: and as is the heavenly, such are they also that are heavenly.”", "Those who belong to each are like their head"),
    ("1Cor 15:49", "“as we have borne the image of the earthy, we shall also bear the image of the heavenly”", "A change of image"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "11.6 The membership of the two heads"),
("p", "Verse 48 shows the membership of the two headships more clearly than any other verse in either passage. Those who belong to the first are called “they also that are earthy.” Those who belong to the second are called “they also that are heavenly.” They are described by being like their head. Nothing is said of what makes a person heavenly, any more than of what makes a person earthy. But the groups are named, and they are two. The thesis of the volume, that “in Adam” and “in Christ” name two headships with different membership, is read straight from these verses."),
]
